  {"type": "text", "h2": "Why the NRCan qualified list matters", "html": "<p>The Home Renovation Savings Program only pays on cold climate air-source heat pumps listed on Natural Resources Canada's qualified products list. The list isn't paperwork for its own sake. To get on it, a model has to prove tested performance in cold conditions, so it doubles as a filter against units that call themselves cold climate on the box and quit at -12°C.</p><p>Two practical notes: pre-approval has to be in place before installation, so confirm eligibility first, and program terms can change, so we check the list and the current rules on every quote.</p>"},
  {"type": "text", "h2": "What's the best cold climate heat pump for Ontario?", "html": "<p>Honest answer: the best cold climate heat pump for Ontario is the one on the NRCan qualified list that's sized correctly for your home's heat loss, matched to the right indoor unit, and installed with the charge, airflow and electrical done properly. A premium model that's oversized will short cycle and underperform a mid-range unit sized right.</p><p>Within that, we install the Midea EVOX G3 most often because it combines a -30°C rating, ENERGY STAR cold climate certification and R-454B refrigerant at a price that leaves room for the rebate to matter. Kepler is our pick when quiet operation or a 1.5 ton size is the priority. We run the heat-loss numbers before we quote.</p>"},
